= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

On its specified domain of admissible functions, a <self-adjoint operator> equals its adjoint, including the adjoint domain. Its integration identity on the interval is
$$
\boxed{\langle u,Lv\rangle=\langle Lu,v\rangle,\qquad\langle u,v\rangle=\int_a^b u^*(x)v(x)\,dx.}
$$
For a differential operator, the boundary conditions must make the boundary form from <integration by parts> vanish. Formal symmetry of the differential expression alone does not specify a self-adjoint boundary-value problem.

Apply this identity to two <eigenfunctions>. Since the weight is real,
$$
\lambda_n\int_a^b wy_m^*y_n\,dx=\int_a^b y_m^*Ly_n\,dx=\int_a^b(Ly_m)^*y_n\,dx=\lambda_m^*\int_a^b wy_m^*y_n\,dx.
$$
First take $m=n$: positivity of $\int_a^b w|y_n|^2dx$ shows $\lambda_n=\lambda_n^*$, so the <eigenvalues> are real. For distinct eigenvalues, subtraction therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\int_a^b w(x)y_m^*(x)y_n(x)\,dx=0\quad(\lambda_m\ne\lambda_n).}
$$
This is <orthogonality of Sturm-Liouville eigenfunctions> in the <weighted inner product>, rather than necessarily in the unweighted inner product.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

To obtain <Sturm-Liouville form>, multiply the equation by $(1-x^2)^{-1/2}$. The derivative identity
$$
\frac d{dx}\big[\sqrt{1-x^2}\,y_n'\big]=\sqrt{1-x^2}\,y_n''-\frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}y_n'
$$
gives
$$
\boxed{-\frac d{dx}\left[\sqrt{1-x^2}\,y_n'\right]=n^2\frac{y_n}{\sqrt{1-x^2}},\qquad p(x)=\sqrt{1-x^2},\quad q(x)=0,\quad w(x)=\frac1{\sqrt{1-x^2}}.}
$$
The weight determines the <weighted inner product> on $(-1,1)$. It is integrable but singular at the endpoints, where $p$ also vanishes. Thus this is a singular-endpoint <Sturm-Liouville problem>, not a regular problem with strictly positive endpoint values of $p$. The transformation in part (ii) specifies its admissible Dirichlet realization; these are the <Dirichlet eigenfunctions of the Chebyshev differential operator>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $Y(\theta)=y(\cos\theta)$ for $0\leq\theta\leq\pi$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
y'=-\frac{Y'}{\sin\theta},\qquad y''=\frac{Y''}{\sin^2\theta}-\frac{\cos\theta}{\sin^3\theta}Y'.
$$
Substitution cancels the first-derivative terms and leaves $-Y''=n^2Y$. The endpoint conditions become $Y(0)=Y(\pi)=0$. For $n\ne0$, the first condition selects $Y=A\sin(n\theta)$, and the second requires $\sin(n\pi)=0$. Thus a nonzero solution requires an integer $n$. The case $n=0$ gives $Y=A+B\theta$ and both conditions force it to vanish. Negative integers give the same eigenvalues and eigenfunctions up to sign, so take $n=1,2,\ldots$.

For <vector normalization>, use $w(x)dx=-d\theta$:
$$
1=\int_{-1}^1w(x)|y_n(x)|^2dx=|A|^2\int_0^\pi\sin^2(n\theta)d\theta=|A|^2\frac\pi2.
$$
Choosing a positive real normalization constant gives
$$
\boxed{y_n(x)=\sqrt{\frac2\pi}\sin(n\arccos x)=\sqrt{\frac2\pi}\sqrt{1-x^2}\,U_{n-1}(x),\qquad\lambda_n=n^2.}
$$
Here $U_{n-1}$ is the <Chebyshev polynomial of the second kind>. The factors $\sqrt{1-x^2}$ make both endpoint values zero. The derivatives need not stay finite there; instead $py_n'=-n\sqrt{2/\pi}\cos(n\theta)$ has finite endpoint limits, ensuring that the relevant boundary form vanishes.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Using the normalized <eigenfunctions> and the same change of variables, their <weighted inner product> is
$$
\int_{-1}^1\frac{y_m(x)y_n(x)}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\,dx=\frac2\pi\int_0^\pi\sin(m\theta)\sin(n\theta)\,d\theta.
$$
For distinct positive integers $m,n$, the product-to-sum identity gives
$$
\frac1\pi\int_0^\pi[\cos((m-n)\theta)-\cos((m+n)\theta)]d\theta=0,
$$
because each integrated sine has integer-multiple-of-$\pi$ endpoints. For $m=n$, the integral is one by the normalization already calculated. Hence
$$
\boxed{\int_{-1}^1w(x)y_m(x)y_n(x)\,dx=\delta_{mn}.}
$$
This verifies <orthonormality> explicitly, with $\delta_{mn}$ the <Kronecker delta>.

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
