= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the <product rule> for the <vector field> $F=\phi\nabla\psi-\psi\nabla\phi$:
$$
\nabla\cdot F=\nabla\phi\cdot\nabla\psi+\phi\nabla^2\psi-\nabla\psi\cdot\nabla\phi-\psi\nabla^2\phi=\phi\nabla^2\psi-\psi\nabla^2\phi.
$$
The mixed <dot products> cancel. Applying the <divergence theorem> with the outward <unit normal> gives <Green's second identity>,
$$
\boxed{\int_V(\phi\nabla^2\psi-\psi\nabla^2\phi)dV=\int_S(\phi\,n\cdot\nabla\psi-\psi\,n\cdot\nabla\phi)dS.}
$$
The fields and boundary are assumed sufficiently regular; a Green-function singularity is handled by excising a small ball or by using <distributions>.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Fix $\mathbf x'$ inside $V$ and put $\psi(\mathbf x)=G(\mathbf x,\mathbf x')$ in <Green's second identity>. The volume integral becomes
$$
\int_V\big[\phi(\mathbf x)(-\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf x-\mathbf x'))-G(\mathbf x,\mathbf x')\cdot0\big]dV=-\phi(\mathbf x').
$$
Here <Laplace's equation> eliminates the second term, and the <Dirac delta> evaluates the first. Reversing the signs in the boundary integral gives
$$
\boxed{\phi(\mathbf x')=\int_S\left[G(\mathbf x,\mathbf x')\,n\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf x}\phi(\mathbf x)-\phi(\mathbf x)\,n\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf x}G(\mathbf x,\mathbf x')\right]dS.}
$$
All boundary derivatives act on the integration variable $\mathbf x$, not on the fixed evaluation point $\mathbf x'$.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Impose the homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary condition> on the boundary variable:
$$
\boxed{G(\mathbf x,\mathbf x')=0\quad\text{for }\mathbf x\in S,\ \mathbf x'\in V.}
$$
This removes the term containing the unknown normal derivative of $\phi$. The prescribed boundary values alone then determine the solution through
$$
\phi(\mathbf x')=-\int_S f(\mathbf x)\,\partial_{n_{\mathbf x}}G(\mathbf x,\mathbf x')\,dS.
$$
For an unbounded domain one also imposes the appropriate decay condition at infinity. This defines the required <Dirichlet Green function>.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let the evaluation point be $\mathbf r_0=(x,y,z)$, $z>0$, and reflect it to $\mathbf r_0^*=(x,y,-z)$. The <method of images> constructs
$$
\boxed{G(\mathbf r,\mathbf r_0)=\frac1{4\pi}\left[\frac1{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0|}-\frac1{|\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0^*|}\right].}
$$
The two <Euclidean distances> coincide on the plane, so $G=0$ there. The negative image source lies outside the domain; inside, only the real source contributes to $-\nabla_{\mathbf r}^2G=\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf r-\mathbf r_0)$. The difference also decays at infinity.

On the plane write $\mathbf r=(\xi,\eta,s)$ and set $s=0$. The outward <unit normal> of the upper half-space is $n=-e_z$. Direct differentiation gives
$$
\left.\partial_sG\right|_{s=0}=\frac{z}{2\pi[(x-\xi)^2+(y-\eta)^2+z^2]^{3/2}},\qquad\partial_nG=-\partial_sG.
$$
Substituting into the boundary representation from part (c) yields the <Poisson kernel for the upper half-space>:
$$
\boxed{\phi(x,y,z)=\frac{z}{2\pi}\int_{\mathbb R^2}\frac{f(\xi,\eta)}{[(x-\xi)^2+(y-\eta)^2+z^2]^{3/2}}\,d\xi\,d\eta.}
$$
Its sign is positive, as expected from the <maximum principle> for nonnegative boundary data. Its normalization can be checked in <polar coordinates>:
$$
\int_{\mathbb R^2}P_z\,d\xi\,d\eta=z\int_0^\infty\frac{r\,dr}{(r^2+z^2)^{3/2}}=1.
$$
As $z\downarrow0$ the kernel is an <approximate identity>, recovering $f$ at its continuity points. Boundary data must have the decay and regularity compatible with the stated condition at infinity.

= e
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

On the axis, the <Poisson kernel for the upper half-space> depends only on the radial boundary coordinate. Integrating over the disk gives
$$
\phi(0,0,z)=\frac z{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}d\theta\int_0^a\frac{r\,dr}{(r^2+z^2)^{3/2}}=z\left[-\frac1{\sqrt{r^2+z^2}}\right]_0^a.
$$
Therefore the <axial Poisson extension of a circular boundary patch> is
$$
\boxed{\phi(0,0,z)=1-\frac{z}{\sqrt{a^2+z^2}},\qquad z>0.}
$$
It tends to one as the axis approaches the boundary inside the patch, and tends to zero as $a^2/(2z^2)$ far above it.

The boundary values jump at the circular rim. They are understood almost everywhere, or as limits at continuity points; changing the assigned value on that measure-zero circle does not change the integral. At a rim point the vertical limit is one-half, rather than a continuous extension with the specified value on both sides of the jump.

